Layer 3: fault control

Faults are how scenarios earn their keep. Every fault in Resonance
works from outside the binary. The binary never cooperates, never
gets a test hook called, never knows it is being tested. There are
five mechanisms, and every check/ scenario is built from them. YAML
scenarios use the same five through do steps (drop, heal,
move, kill, restart) in suite/exec.c.

1. Packet drop (partition)

The workhorse. Instead of pumping a TUN file descriptor, read its
packet and throw it away:

  n = read(fd, junk, sizeof(junk));   /* drop */
  tun_pump_fd(map, fd, grid, radio, nodes);  /* forward */

check/concord_partition.c:28-41 shows the check/-only shape.
Eighteen check/ Concord files use it with different predicates
(counted with grep -ln 'read(fd, junk' check/concord_*.c): one
node isolated by index (the common i == ISOLATE form), all
descriptors dropped (full cut in partition, three-way split in
splitbrain and orphan_partition), per-node arrays with a rotating
victim (spam), an elected victim (duel), or a dead leader whose
packets blackhole forever (failover). Reunion is simply resuming
the pump. Nothing on the binary side changes state; the world just
goes quiet and comes back. YAML scenarios use drop plus heal
steps for the same effect.

2. Multicast blinding

Pump everything except IPv4 multicast. mDNS discovery goes blind
while unicast gossip, sync, and Noise keep flowing. Only
check/concord_anchor.c:34 uses it, to prove that provisioned
rendezvous anchors carry discovery alone. That reference is
check/-only. If you need the one-line version: this fault unplugs
the LAN's shouting channel and checks the phone book still works.

3. Moving nodes (radio truth)

Change a node's simulated position and let radio_path do the
partitioning. move_node in check/concord_mobile.c:36-51 drives
node 2 from 2 m (X_HOME) to 7 m (X_AWAY), past the 2.5 m range,
and back, without any file descriptor ever being dropped. The static
version is check/tun_drop.c:33-42: one node parked ten meters out
so physics drops everything while the pump runs normally. Both
references are check/-only; YAML scenarios use the move step with
x_m for the same effect. Prefer moves over drops when the property
under test is about radio reality (range, mobility, reunion without
reconnects) rather than about packet loss.

4. Lag batching

pump_slow in check/concord_slow.c:32-46 drains a node's packets
only once every SLOW_SECS (2 s). Nothing is lost; everything is
late. Only check/concord_slow uses it, and no YAML scenario uses a
slow-subscriber shape. It proves convergence tolerates a laggy
link with flapping suspicion. A slow-subscriber scenario (for
example Weld backpressure) stays unimplemented; that scenario reuses
this mechanism.

5. Killing processes

Final cleanup everywhere uses SIGTERM. Faults use SIGKILL, except the
key-rotation restarts, which use SIGTERM because the node cooperates
by rotating first. The kill matrix (who dies, when, restarted which
way) is in /docs/layer2-lifecycle. From this layer's view a kill
is just another partition with a process attached: the node's packets
stop because nobody reads them, and restart reattaches it. YAML
scenarios use kill plus restart steps with a named durability.

Timers that schedule faults

Three timer flavors appear in check/ scenarios, all built on the
same arms-on-first-call pattern: store t0 on first call, fire N
seconds later. Twenty-six check/ files carry that pattern as a
byte-identical hold() helper, frozen there; the rest use the same
pattern inline (for example check/concord_restart.c:95-102). The
shared run_hold (run/run.h:95, body in run/run.c:351-358)
mirrors the same semantics for new code. YAML
scenarios use hold_s plus timeout_s keys instead, run by
run_hold inside suite/exec.c.

- HOLD_UP (usually 8 s, check/-only): mesh must be up and settled
  before the fault lands. Never skip it; every reunion verdict
  assumes a meshed start. The YAML form is hold_s on a mesh
  phase.
- HOLD_DOWN (usually 10 s) and friends (HOLD_SPLIT 90 s,
  ISOLATE_SECS 12 s, all check/-only): how long the fault lasts.
  The YAML form is sustain with timeout_s.
- DEADLINE (90 to 600 s, check/-only): the backstop. If the verdict
  is not reached by then, the scenario fails instead of hanging
  forever. The YAML form is timeout_s per phase plus file-level
  timeout_s.

The rule

Faults arrive through pump predicates, node positions, and signals
only. If a fault needs the binary's cooperation, it is not a fault,
it is a feature request. File it against the system under test.
